  "raw": "{\n  \"audits\": [\n    {\n      \"questionId\": \"q001\",\n      \"decision\": \"accept\",\n      \"assessedIds\": [\"cnm_p105\"],\n      \"supportIds\": [],\n      \"routes\": [],\n      \"derivedAnswer\": \"A — One pair of parallel opposite sides.\",\n      \"mathematicalReason\": \"Under the exclusive definition used by the question and cnm_p105, a trapezoid has exactly one pair of parallel opposite sides. This directly matches the node's trapezoid criterion.\",\n      \"candidateFindings\": [],\n      \"needsNewNodes\": [],\n      \"limitations\": [],\n      \"figureEvidence\": [\n        {\n          \"imageSlot\": 1,\n          \"fact\": \"Matching arrow marks identify the upper and lower opposite sides as parallel.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"questionId\": \"q002\",\n      \"decision\": \"accept\",\n      \"assessedIds\": [\"cnm_p113\"],\n      \"supportIds\": [],\n      \"routes\": [],\n      \"derivedAnswer\": \"A — The shapes seen may be different.\",\n      \"mathematicalReason\": \"Different viewing directions can produce different outlines or proportions for the same object; identical views are not guaranteed. This directly matches cnm_p113's criterion that shapes seen from different directions may differ, without requiring reconstruction of the solid.\",\n      \"candidateFindings\": [],\n      \"needsNewNodes\": [],\n      \"limitations\": [],\n      \"figureEvidence\": [\n        {\n          \"imageSlot\": 2,\n          \"fact\": \"The views labeled Front, Side, and Top are rectangles drawn with different proportions.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"questionId\": \"q003\",\n      \"decision\": \"accept\",\n      \"assessedIds\": [\"cnm_p149\"],\n      \"supportIds\": [],\n      \"routes\": [],\n      \"derivedAnswer\": \"16 cm²\",\n      \"mathematicalReason\": \"The trapezoid's area is (2 + 6) × 4 ÷ 2 = 16 cm². The given 4 cm is the perpendicular height, not a sloping side. Identifying the bases and height and applying this formula directly matches cnm_p149.\",\n      \"candidateFindings\": [],\n      \"needsNewNodes\": [],\n      \"limitations\": [],\n      \"figureEvidence\": [\n        {\n          \"imageSlot\": 3,\n          \"fact\": \"The upper and lower bases are labeled 2 cm and 6 cm; the interior segment labeled 4 cm has a right-angle mark where it meets the lower base.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"questionId\": \"q004\",\n      \"decision\": \"accept\",\n      \"assessedIds\": [\"cnm_h111\"],\n      \"supportIds\": [],\n      \"routes\": [],\n      \"derivedAnswer\": \"9 cubic units\",\n      \"mathematicalReason\": \"A cone has volume V = Bh/3, so V = 9 × 3 ÷ 3 = 9. The supplied definition of cnm_h111 explicitly includes calculating cone volume from known base area and height; no radius calculation is needed.\",\n      \"candidateFindings\": [],\n      \"needsNewNodes\": [],\n      \"limitations\": [],\n      \"figureEvidence\": [\n        {\n          \"imageSlot\": 4,\n          \"fact\": \"The cone is labeled Base area 9 and Height 3, with an interior height segment drawn from the apex toward the base.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"questionId\": \"q005\",\n      \"decision\": \"accept\",\n      \"assessedIds\": [\"cnm_p150\"],\n      \"supportIds\": [\"cnm_174\", \"cnm_p148\"],\n      \"routes\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"similar-right-triangles\",\n          \"label\": \"Similar right triangles\",\n          \"nodeIds\": [\"cnm_271\", \"cnm_272\"]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"perpendicular-bisector-equation\",\n          \"label\": \"Equal distances and a length equation\",\n          \"nodeIds\": [\"cnm_133\", \"cnm_148\", \"cnm_042\"]\n        }\n      ],\n      \"derivedAnswer\": \"B — 58½ square units.\",\n      \"mathematicalReason\": \"Since C is a right angle, BC = √(20² − 12²) = 16 and area(ABC) = 12 × 16/2 = 96. Also BD = 10. For the similarity route, triangles BDE and BCA have a common angle at B and right angles at D and C, so DE/AC = BD/BC = 10/16; hence DE = 7.5. Thus area(ADEC) = 96 − 10 × 7.5/2 = 58.5. The alternative route is also valid: DE is the perpendicular bisector of AB, so EA = EB. Writing EB = x gives 12² + (16 − x)² = x², which reduces to x = 12.5, within segment BC. Pythagoras then gives the positive length DE = √(12.5² − 10²) = 7.5. Subtracting triangle areas matches cnm_p150, with Pythagoras and triangle area supporting both displayed routes. The two routes are alternatives, not jointly required methods.\",\n      \"candidateFindings\": [],\n      \"needsNewNodes\": [],\n      \"limitations\": [],\n      \"figureEvidence\": [\n        {\n          \"imageSlot\": 5,\n          \"fact\": \"D lies on segment AB, E lies on segment CB, and the right-angle mark at D shows DE perpendicular to AB. Segment DE divides triangle ABC into quadrilateral ADEC and triangle BDE.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"questionId\": \"q006\",\n      \"decision\": \"accept\",\n      \"assessedIds\": [\"cnm_p188\"],\n      \"supportIds\": [],\n      \"routes\": [],\n      \"derivedAnswer\": \"A — The distances all equal the radius.\",\n      \"mathematicalReason\": \"A circle consists of points at a fixed distance r from its center. Therefore every center-to-circle distance equals the radius. This is explicitly included in cnm_p188's criteria.\",\n      \"candidateFindings\": [],\n      \"needsNewNodes\": [],\n      \"limitations\": [],\n      \"figureEvidence\": [\n        {\n          \"imageSlot\": 6,\n          \"fact\": \"Three segments join the marked center to the circumference, carry matching equality ticks, and include a radius label r.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"questionId\": \"q007\",\n      \"decision\": \"accept\",\n      \"assessedIds\": [\"cnm_p207\"],\n      \"supportIds\": [\"cnm_p192\", \"cnm_p132\"],\n      \"routes\": [],\n      \"derivedAnswer\": \"31.4 cm³\",\n      \"mathematicalReason\": \"Using the prescribed value π = 314/100 = 3.14, the circular base area is πr² = 3.14 × 1² = 3.14 cm². Multiplying by the height gives V = 3.14 × 10 = 31.4 cm³. This matches cnm_p207, with circle area and decimal-by-integer multiplication as valid supporting skills for this calculation. Decimal arithmetic is a valid selected method, not the only possible arithmetic method.\",\n      \"candidateFindings\": [],\n      \"needsNewNodes\": [],\n      \"limitations\": [],\n      \"figureEvidence\": [\n        {\n          \"imageSlot\": 7,\n          \"fact\": \"The cylinder is labeled r = 1 cm and h = 10 cm; the top segment runs from the marked center to the rim, identifying the given length as a radius.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"questionId\": \"q008\",\n      \"decision\": \"accept\",\n      \"assessedIds\": [\"cnm_h111\"],\n      \"supportIds\": [],\n      \"routes\": [],\n      \"derivedAnswer\": \"80 cubic units\",\n      \"mathematicalReason\": \"A prism's volume is its base area multiplied by its perpendicular height: V = Bh = 20 × 4 = 80. Calculating prism volume from known base area and height directly matches the supplied definition of cnm_h111.\",\n      \"candidateFindings\": [],\n      \"needsNewNodes\": [],\n      \"limitations\": [],\n      \"figureEvidence\": [\n        {\n          \"imageSlot\": 8,\n          \"fact\": \"The pictured prism is labeled Base area 20 and Height 4; no individual base-edge lengths are needed.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}",
